WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.400
So the section we're going to be kicking off right now

00:02.400 --> 00:05.160
is on watchers and computed properties.

00:05.160 --> 00:07.400
We've talked a lot about reactive data,

00:07.400 --> 00:10.760
and we saw that it's cool, that it tracks.

00:10.760 --> 00:13.720
But the next step to reactive data a lot of times

00:13.720 --> 00:17.080
comes to the point where it's a question of what happens

00:17.080 --> 00:19.440
when things depend on that data, right?

00:19.440 --> 00:21.320
So you want something to happen when

00:21.320 --> 00:22.640
the reactive data changes.

00:22.640 --> 00:24.200
This is actually fairly common.

00:24.200 --> 00:26.800
So granted, we're doing a lot of things automatically

00:26.800 --> 00:27.600
at this point, right?

00:27.600 --> 00:29.800
So for example, when the data updates view

00:29.800 --> 00:31.600
will automatically update the HTML.

00:31.600 --> 00:34.520
But what do we want to do more fine-grained things?

00:34.520 --> 00:38.720
So for example, we want to watch the count up here.

00:38.720 --> 00:42.960
And if it's even, we want to go ahead and then, say,

00:42.960 --> 00:46.800
update the title up here to something else, right?

00:46.800 --> 00:49.960
So you can imagine that if we had this to be counter title,

00:49.960 --> 00:55.080
for example, and counter title would just put it here.

00:55.080 --> 00:59.200
So counter title is going to be just a standard,

00:59.200 --> 01:01.080
for example.

01:01.080 --> 01:01.960
So you can see it in a fresh.

01:01.960 --> 01:03.720
Great, counter standard here.

01:03.720 --> 01:05.200
And we want to say we want to watch

01:05.200 --> 01:09.040
for if the count goes past 20, then we

01:09.040 --> 01:12.120
want to add, like, long to the text.

01:12.120 --> 01:14.800
And so your first instinct for those who come from a framework

01:14.800 --> 01:17.360
background is to think, well, we need to be able to watch

01:17.360 --> 01:18.520
the thing, right?

01:18.520 --> 01:21.680
And so to watch something in view is actually fairly simple.

01:21.680 --> 01:25.080
There actually is a watch option, as you would expect.

01:25.080 --> 01:26.720
So we have data, we have methods.

01:26.720 --> 01:29.040
Here's our opportunity to watch something.

01:29.040 --> 01:30.160
And when you want to watch something,

01:30.160 --> 01:33.560
you basically say, what is it exactly that we're watching?

01:33.560 --> 01:36.760
Well, in this case, we're watching the count.

01:36.760 --> 01:39.240
And so when you actually define the count, though,

01:39.240 --> 01:41.080
what you want to do is you want to say,

01:41.080 --> 01:42.480
you need the value that is basically

01:42.480 --> 01:44.000
going to return to you by default.

01:44.000 --> 01:45.000
Because otherwise, you don't have

01:45.000 --> 01:48.200
something to refer to inside of that value.

01:48.200 --> 01:50.400
Because it's going to basically say, what's your new value?

01:50.400 --> 01:51.440
What's your old value?

01:51.440 --> 01:53.720
And so by default, you get the new value as the first one.

01:53.720 --> 01:56.400
So let me actually just do new value like this.

01:56.400 --> 02:00.080
So you'll see now, here we have a simple watch function

02:00.080 --> 02:01.400
that's watching count.

02:01.400 --> 02:05.680
And then when it changes, we should automatically see it log.

02:05.680 --> 02:12.360
And so, ooh, do, do, do, do, watch count, did not refresh.

02:12.360 --> 02:13.520
Refreshing would be helpful.

02:13.520 --> 02:13.960
There we go.

02:13.960 --> 02:14.560
OK.

02:14.560 --> 02:17.760
So we can see here, 1826, we're getting some logs as well here

02:17.760 --> 02:18.880
from increment count.

02:18.880 --> 02:21.640
But we see that at least it's being triggered.

02:21.640 --> 02:24.480
So your next thing would be like, OK, well, logically speaking,

02:24.480 --> 02:26.280
what I would want to do then is I would say,

02:26.320 --> 02:32.200
well, if new value is greater than 20, then this dot counter

02:32.200 --> 02:39.240
title, ooh, counter title plus equals very long.

02:39.240 --> 02:40.560
OK.

02:40.560 --> 02:43.400
Now, refresh, one, two.

02:43.400 --> 02:45.520
You can see, boom, it's updated.

02:45.520 --> 02:47.880
And so from a mental model perspective, you're like, yeah,

02:47.880 --> 02:48.600
that makes sense.

02:48.600 --> 02:49.680
Cool.

02:49.680 --> 02:52.560
And so this is actually a common paradigm in a lot of frameworks

02:52.560 --> 02:54.520
when it comes to watching reactive dependencies.

02:54.520 --> 02:56.160
That's what it's called.

02:56.160 --> 02:59.760
But view has a better way for us to do this, especially when

02:59.760 --> 03:02.040
it comes to modifying existing data.

03:02.040 --> 03:04.360
Because if you think about what we're doing here, what we're

03:04.360 --> 03:06.680
doing is we're saying, based on a reactive data property,

03:06.680 --> 03:08.960
run a specific computation.

03:08.960 --> 03:11.840
And then, but if we think about that from a performance

03:11.840 --> 03:13.680
perspective, we really only wanted

03:13.680 --> 03:16.760
to do that when all the dependent, like the right

03:16.760 --> 03:18.200
dependencies are triggered.

03:18.200 --> 03:20.600
And so one of the problems that comes with doing something

03:20.600 --> 03:24.520
as explicitly watching values is sometimes, especially when

03:24.560 --> 03:27.400
apps get really large, the watch can end up

03:27.400 --> 03:31.000
getting triggered like a lot, like unnecessarily so.

03:31.000 --> 03:33.120
And so when you hear things about people accidentally

03:33.120 --> 03:36.120
triggering re-renders, that's what they're talking about.

03:36.120 --> 03:38.280
It's because at that point, you're manually

03:38.280 --> 03:39.440
watching the dependencies.

03:39.440 --> 03:40.760
And if you don't know what you're doing,

03:40.760 --> 03:42.840
or not caching it correctly, all of a sudden,

03:42.840 --> 03:45.360
you might be triggering re-rendering of thousands

03:45.360 --> 03:47.720
of nodes and whatnot.

03:47.720 --> 03:50.120
So view, in its view fashion, came up

03:50.120 --> 03:53.240
with an API that would help to abstract that away.

03:53.240 --> 03:55.680
And the way it does that is through the idea

03:55.680 --> 03:57.880
of computed properties.

03:57.880 --> 03:59.520
And so the way computed properties worked

03:59.520 --> 04:03.280
is basically another option on your app.

04:03.280 --> 04:06.080
So I'm going to add it below data because,

04:06.080 --> 04:08.080
and you'll see shortly why.

04:08.080 --> 04:10.280
So computed properties is think of it

04:10.280 --> 04:12.760
as basically performance optimized calculations

04:12.760 --> 04:15.400
on reactive data that you have.

04:15.400 --> 04:19.320
So in the instance of, for example, the counter title,

04:19.320 --> 04:21.920
rather than it be like, OK, well, I'm

04:21.920 --> 04:23.640
going to have a watch on this count.

04:23.640 --> 04:25.800
If it's 20, then add the thing.

04:25.800 --> 04:30.280
Instead, you could be, I'll say, display title, for example.

04:30.280 --> 04:35.360
And so this will be a function that view will basically,

04:35.360 --> 04:37.240
it will correctly decide when to run it.

04:37.240 --> 04:38.640
So we'll see shortly.

04:38.640 --> 04:40.400
And in this function, what are we going to do?

04:40.400 --> 04:45.640
We're going to say, if this dot count is greater than 20,

04:45.640 --> 04:49.760
return counter standard.

04:49.760 --> 04:52.920
Otherwise, return, oh wait, in this case,

04:52.920 --> 04:55.440
it was counter very long.

04:55.440 --> 05:00.880
Otherwise, return counter standard, just like that.

05:00.880 --> 05:09.600
And so we switch this over now to display title and refresh.

05:09.600 --> 05:11.560
You see nothing changes, and then it's swapped,

05:11.560 --> 05:13.000
and then it's good.

05:13.000 --> 05:16.040
And basically, even though you might think programmatically,

05:16.040 --> 05:18.560
this looks very similar to what we just did,

05:18.560 --> 05:20.960
this is extremely powerful, because what you've just done

05:20.960 --> 05:24.160
is now given view the job of figuring out

05:24.160 --> 05:26.480
when its dependencies have changed.

05:26.480 --> 05:28.120
And so to show you what I mean by that,

05:28.120 --> 05:31.280
we could even combine computed properties together.

05:31.280 --> 05:32.880
So for example, let's say that we actually

05:32.880 --> 05:35.480
wanted to impact the increment amount based

05:35.480 --> 05:36.880
on the length of the title.

05:36.880 --> 05:38.040
Let's just do that.

05:38.040 --> 05:41.880
So we could then say, let's say I call it

05:41.880 --> 05:44.200
optimize increment amount.

05:44.200 --> 05:46.120
And for whatever reason, we have some algorithm

05:46.120 --> 05:48.520
that's dependent on the counter title.

05:48.520 --> 05:54.160
What we could do is that we can return this dot display

05:54.160 --> 05:59.280
title dot length times, and then, let's say,

05:59.280 --> 06:00.840
this dot increment amount.

06:03.440 --> 06:05.200
So now we have two dependencies.

06:05.200 --> 06:07.800
We're depending on this display title here.

06:07.800 --> 06:11.160
We're dependent on increment amount here.

06:11.160 --> 06:16.040
But then once we update here to optimize increment amount,

06:16.040 --> 06:20.040
we can see now that things are starting to change actually

06:20.040 --> 06:21.720
pretty drastically.

06:21.720 --> 06:24.360
You're seeing that the, oh, actually, I've realized you're

06:24.360 --> 06:26.080
not actually seeing the optimize amount.

06:26.080 --> 06:28.240
So let's show that real quick.

06:28.240 --> 06:29.480
Where's my increment count?

06:29.480 --> 06:30.760
OK, here we go.

06:30.760 --> 06:34.760
Then we can say optimize increment amount.

06:34.760 --> 06:37.760
Very good.

06:37.760 --> 06:39.760
So you can see here it's 224 right here.

06:39.760 --> 06:41.520
And so it's changing because why?

06:41.520 --> 06:43.880
Because we've modified the title.

06:43.880 --> 06:46.000
If we modify this times 10, you'll

06:46.000 --> 06:48.400
see that now it's updating correctly by the increment

06:48.400 --> 06:50.600
amount, but the optimize amount is now

06:50.600 --> 06:52.840
dependent on two different things.

06:52.840 --> 06:54.680
And so what you get with computer properties

06:54.680 --> 06:56.960
is basically really efficient tool chaining

06:56.960 --> 06:59.120
on reactive dependencies.

06:59.120 --> 07:01.560
And this is a concept we're starting to see popularized.

07:01.560 --> 07:04.240
I think Svelte has something very similar as well now,

07:04.240 --> 07:07.240
in that if you think about an enterprise level app,

07:07.240 --> 07:09.880
there is going to be so much that's changing.

07:09.880 --> 07:12.720
And when you have manual definitions of what you're

07:12.720 --> 07:15.480
watching, oftentimes you'll find that it's actually fairly

07:15.480 --> 07:18.280
brittle because one small change will break

07:18.280 --> 07:19.800
that entire dependency chain.

07:19.800 --> 07:21.840
Whereas here, you can have your source of truth

07:21.840 --> 07:24.040
being this data store, and then you

07:24.040 --> 07:27.360
can have all your computations run off of that thing.

07:27.360 --> 07:29.200
And it knows when to trigger, it knows what to do,

07:29.200 --> 07:31.400
it knows how to cache it, so that in the HTML

07:31.400 --> 07:33.080
it doesn't randomly trigger.

07:33.080 --> 07:36.920
All that stuff that our goal is to build cool things

07:36.920 --> 07:39.720
and to abstract business logic, deliver value to customers.

07:39.720 --> 07:41.920
As much as it's fun to learn about performance optimizations,

07:41.920 --> 07:44.520
that's not something we want to be debugging constantly.

07:44.520 --> 07:47.200
And so computer properties are incredibly powerful for this.

07:47.200 --> 07:51.680
In fact, if I were to say my single most favorite feature

07:51.680 --> 07:53.520
of view, it's the computer property

07:53.520 --> 07:56.000
because it just takes away all the worry for me

07:56.000 --> 07:58.640
about what's happening, and it does so much work for me.

07:58.640 --> 08:00.280
Again, really easy.

08:00.280 --> 08:02.400
And it's particularly powerful if you

08:02.400 --> 08:03.600
do any data visualizations.

08:03.600 --> 08:04.480
You can imagine, right?

08:04.480 --> 08:06.240
You're doing all these SVG calculations

08:06.240 --> 08:08.080
based on a set group of data.

08:08.080 --> 08:10.840
You don't want to have to write the dependency chain that

08:10.840 --> 08:12.720
would update the SVG at this particular point,

08:12.720 --> 08:15.280
and then it swaps this way or how it animates.

08:15.280 --> 08:18.280
All that can be managed basically with computer properties.

08:18.280 --> 08:20.360
And so some of the most elegant stores I've seen

08:20.360 --> 08:23.080
have one source of truth inside of data,

08:23.080 --> 08:25.000
and then they compute everything else out.

08:25.000 --> 08:27.880
And it just works really, really well

08:27.880 --> 08:30.080
because view can optimize all those things.

